There are other versions of mutations. We can consider any embedded surface in $M$ with a special symmetry such as the four-punctured sphere above. Cutting $M$ along such a surface, applying the symmetry and regluing leads to a mutated manifold. For example, we can cut along a genus 2 surface without punctures and use the $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$ hyperelliptic involution. Every genus 2 conformal block is invariant under the corresponding $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$ symmetry acting by a rotation around the $x$ axis as follows:77 7 This would fail at genus 3 since we have in general two different Liouville momenta on the bottom and top of the middle loop, which get exchanged by this operation. Correspondingly, not every genus 3 surface is hyperelliptic.

