So we see that applying the statistics (90) precisely reproduces the result (44) anticipated from the Gaussian ensemble.

Notice that in this case the corresponding sixth moment receives contributions from configurations in which the Wilson lines have a different pattern of over- and under-crossings in the bulk, in addition to those with higher topology in the bulk. In principle, we could consider contributions from the manifolds formed by cutting $M$ along a six-punctured sphere in the bulk and gluing in another six-punctured sphere with any tangle formed by three strands in the bulk. As a simple example, we could have considered the following six-boundary wormhole

