It is also simple to compute the volumes in a large $p$ and $q$ expansion, since for large $p$ or $q$ , the saddle point $\eta^{*}\to 0$ and the volume converges to the volume of the figure eight knot. We find to the first few orders

$$ \text{vol}(4_{1}(p,q))=\text{vol}(4_{1})-\frac{2\sqrt{3}\pi^{2}}{p^{2}+12q^{2}}+\frac{4\pi^{4}(p^{4}-72p^{2}q^{2}+144q^{4})}{\sqrt{3}(p^{2}+12q^{2})^{4}}\\
-\frac{8\pi^{6}(23p^{8}-8904p^{6}q^{2}+302400p^{4}q^{4}-1620864p^{2}q^{6}+767232q^{8})}{45\sqrt{3}(p^{2}+12q^{2})^{7}}+\cdots $$

The correction to the figure eight knot volume is always negative as required by general theorems about Dehn surgery (54, Theorem 6.5.6.). This expansion is a known result, see [55]. This case of Dehn surgery exemplifies the existence of accumulation points in the spectrum of three-manifolds. We discussed their implications for the gravitational path integral in our previous paper [1].

